<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Musasir</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 714 BCE (approx.) · capital Musasir</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small mountain city-state, sacred centre of the Urartian god Haldi, whose temple was plundered by Sargon II of Assyria in 714 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Musasir, called Ardini by the Urartians, lay in the mountains between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Urartu</a>, probably near modern Mudjesir in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a> Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. It appears in Assyrian texts by the 9th century BCE and was ruled by its own kings while being bound to the Kingdom of Urartu by religion.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city's great temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haldi">Haldi</a>, the chief god of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Urartu</a>, made it a pilgrimage and coronation site for Urartian kings, who dedicated weapons, shields and statues there. Its rulers balanced between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and Urartu; the best known, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urzana">Urzana</a>, wrote to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> while honouring Urartian rites. The kingdom was small, its wealth coming mostly from the temple treasury.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 714 BCE, on his eighth campaign, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> marched on Musasir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urzana">Urzana</a> fled, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> carried off a vast treasure listed in his account; a relief at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorsabad">Khorsabad</a> depicts the temple. The city later recovered as a cult centre but lost its independence.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/musasir.jpg" alt="Musasir" width="320" height="479"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sandstone_statue_of_a_man_or_deity._The_statue_belonged_to_the_Musasir_Kingdom._Urartian_period,_1st_millennium_BCE._Precise_provenance_of_excavation_is_unknown._Erbil_Civilization_Museum,_Iraqi_Kurdistan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 714 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Musasir</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Urzana</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Urartian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Urartian, Hurrian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ardini, Mutsatsir</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-807&amp;focus=musasir" aria-label="Musasir on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5840 878 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Musasir lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6160 1134L6077 1051M6160 1134L6092 1134"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6077 1051h0M6092 1134h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6160 1134h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6160 1134h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-807&amp;focus=musasir">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

